1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,040
I have to admit that when it comes to food, I'm a total sucker.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:08,360
Whether it's sugar or grease or carbs, pretty much bring it on.

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,740
And I spend a lot of time in Montana.

4
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:14,760
So for me, that medium-rare grass-fed rib-eye steak?

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,240
Pretty much as good as it gets.

6
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:17,920
I know, I don't do it often.

7
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:21,140
And when I do, I gotta admit, I feel a little conflicted.

8
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:24,260
And that's for a lot of reasons, including the planet.

9
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,760
But how big of a problem is what I eat?

10
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:31,080
I mean, does it really make much of a dent in something as huge as global warming?

11
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,360
But it turns out what we put on our plates matters a lot.

12
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:45,080
About 25% of all the global climate change problems we're seeing

13
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,440
can be attributed back to the food and the choices

14
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,680
that we're actually making about what we eat on a daily basis.

15
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,120
This is greater than all the cars on the planet.

16
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:57,160
In fact, it's about twice as much global warming pollution as the cars.

17
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:02,000
Ben Holton and Maya Almoroz study the connection between climate and diet

18
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,120
at the University of California, Davis.

19
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:08,080
They track how the way we produce food creates greenhouse gases

20
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:10,000
that contribute to global warming.

21
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,240
With their data, the team has crunched the numbers

22
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,960
to figure out how much carbon pollution is produced

23
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,200
by different foods and different diets.

24
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,880
A lot of people feel really helpless when it comes to climate change,

25
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:21,280
like they can't make a difference.

26
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:24,240
And what our research is showing is that your personal decisions

27
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:26,440
really can have a big impact.

28
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:29,040
So take that grass-fed ribeye steak I love.

29
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:32,520
If you really look at everything that went into making a single serving of beef,

30
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:36,160
you end up emitting around 330 grams of carbon.

31
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:38,520
That's like driving a car three miles.

32
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:40,480
Now, if I choose to have chicken instead,

33
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:42,880
there's more than a five-fold drop in emissions.

34
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:45,920
Switch to fish and you see the number go down even more.

35
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,320
Now, look at veggies.

36
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:50,400
If I swapped beef out entirely for lentils,

37
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:52,200
well, I'm down to practically nothing.

38
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,720
So why does beef, and lamb too for that matter,

39
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:57,720
pack such a powerful punch to the planet?

40
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:03,040
Livestock accounts for a little over 14% of global greenhouse gas emissions.

41
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:04,640
If that sort of seems low to you,

42
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,640
consider it's about equal to transportation.

43
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:11,160
We're talking all the cars, trucks, planes, trains,

44
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,560
and ships on the planet combined.

45
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:17,440
This is partly because ruminant animals like cows and sheep,

46
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,640
they're just gassy.

47
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:25,320
And the methane they produce is at least 25 times more potent than carbon dioxide.

48
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,840
Plus, it takes a lot of land, fertilizer,

49
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:32,160
and about a billion tons of grain to feed all that livestock.

50
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,920
And you could feed 3.5 billion people with that grain.

51
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,640
If we were just directly eating these grains ourselves,

52
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,680
it would eliminate a lot of the CO2 that's emitted from cattle production.

53
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:45,160
So it's clear that meat has a pretty big carbon load,

54
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:49,680
but it's also worth remembering that not all livestock is raised equally.

55
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:51,720
In parts of the American West, for instance,

56
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:56,680
ranchers are working to raise livestock in ways that actually help restore the land.

57
00:02:56,680 --> 00:03:00,400
And they're experimenting with ways that soil and grasslands

58
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,440
can be used to keep carbon pollution out of the air.

59
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:06,440
But even these sustainable ranchers will really tell you,

60
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,600
we're probably eating too much meat.

61
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:12,200
I know a lot of people who, if you don't serve them meat for lunch or dinner,

62
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,320
they're kind of like, well, when is the meat coming out?

63
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,560
It's to the point now where the US actually has one of the highest

64
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:19,080
meat footprints per capita.

65
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:21,520
So what about not eating meat at all?

66
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,360
Vegan is the way to go for the least impact on the planet.

67
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:28,880
But it's not that much different in terms of emissions than, say, a vegetarian diet.

68
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:32,680
And the team found that the environmental impact of the Mediterranean diet

69
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:36,120
is pretty similar to vegan and vegetarian diets.

70
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:39,240
It's a lot less meat heavy than what Americans are used to.

71
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,200
So fish and poultry a few times a week,

72
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:45,760
beef maybe once a month, plenty of plant-based foods,

73
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:47,600
and of course, loads of olive oil.

74
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:50,280
Eliminating, like, 90% of your meat intake

75
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:53,120
is more important than eliminating all of your meat.

76
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:54,320
We don't all have to be vegan.

77
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,200
We don't all even have to be vegetarian.

78
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,960
If we can just reduce our meat intake, every little bit helps.

79
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:01,840
And if you can bring it down a lot, you can help the climate a lot.

80
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:04,680
If we all just switched to a Mediterranean diet,

81
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:09,440
it could actually solve 15% of global warming pollution by 2050.

82
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:11,440
If everyone were to move toward it,

83
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:15,600
that is equivalent to taking somewhere around a billion cars off of the streets

84
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:17,440
in terms of vehicle emissions each year.

85
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,880
So that kind of a footprint is big time.

86
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:23,760
But say you still want more meat than the Mediterranean diet recommends.

87
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:28,320
Just cutting down your portion size to the doctor-recommended four ounces

88
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:30,840
can reduce your emissions by half.

89
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:31,640
That's huge.

90
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:35,200
In fact, the doctors are telling us we're eating about twice as much meat

91
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,320
as we really need for a healthy diet.

92
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:40,080
The good news is we are listening to our doctors.

93
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:44,520
In the last decade, there's been a 19% drop in the amount of beef we eat.

94
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:47,200
All these things that you're already being told are good for you

95
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,200
also happen to be good for the planet.

96
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:52,920
So what we eat really is a big part of the climate puzzle.

97
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:55,920
I mean, we may not all be able to afford an electric car

98
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:58,200
or putting solar panels on our house,

99
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,120
but we all have to eat every day.

100
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:03,280
And these choices we make can add up to really big numbers.

101
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,960
And since meat has a pretty big carbon load,

102
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:08,600
we need to be thoughtful about how much we eat.

103
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:11,000
As for that ribeye steak that I really love,

104
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,600
I am honestly trying to cut back.

105
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:15,840
Maybe it's just a smaller piece of steak

106
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:19,360
or simply swapping out a meat dish for a veggie burger.

107
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:23,160
It may seem like a small thing, but it really does add up to big impacts.

